  <subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integral-polar">
    <title>Double Integrals in Polar Coordinates</title>
    <p>
      When integrating over the <m>xy-</m>plane and using <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as our variables, we divide up the region into tiny rectangles whose base is <m>dx \cdot dy</m>.
      In polar coordinates, the plane is divided up into circular sectors shaped like slices of pizza or pieces of pie. The area of a sector is radius times arc length and\\ 
      <m>A = r\cdot s = r(r\theta)</m> as we shrink the area down to the d-level (calculus!) we get <m>dA = dr(r d\theta)</m>. Since <m>\theta</m> is measured in radians and
      is unitless, <m>r dr d\theta</m> has units of area units squared. <m>f(r,\theta)</m> provides the height and so the double integral 
      <md>
        \int_a^b \int_c^d f(r,\theta) r dr d\theta
      </md>
      calculates a volume. Integrating in polar coordinates simplifies calculations greatly for circular-like regions. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Calculate the double integral
        <md>
          \int\int_{R} (x+y) dA
        </md>
        over the area pictured below. (This region is called an annulus.)
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>An annulus</caption>
        <image source="assets/2dpolar1.png">
          <shortdescription>an annulus or the area between a circle of radius 2 and a circle of radius 4 for angles between zero and pi over 2.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The region can be described in polar coordinates as <m>2\leq r \leq 4</m> and <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. We also need to convert the function
        to polar coordinates using <m>x=r\cos(\theta)</m> and <m>y=r\sin(\theta)</m>. The double integral is then
        <md>
          \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}} \int_2^4 (r\cos(\theta)+r\sin(\theta))r dr d\theta = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}} r^2(\cos(\theta)+\sin(\theta)) dr d\theta =
        </md>
        <md>
          \left( \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}}(\cos(\theta)+\sin(\theta))d\theta\right)\cdot \left(\int_2^4 r^2 dr\right) = \left(\left. -\sin(\theta)+\cos(\theta)\right\vert_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\right)\cdot
          \left(\left. \frac{r^3}{3}\right\vert_2^4\right)
        </md>
        <md>
          =(2)\left(\frac{56}{3}\right) = \frac{112}{3}
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Calculate the double integral
        <md>
          \int\int_R \frac{1}{(x^2+y^2)^2}dA
        </md>
        over the region pictured below:
      </p>
<figure>
  <caption>A quarter of a shifted circle</caption>
  <image source="assets/2dpolar2.png">
    <shortdescription>The top right section of a circle of radius 1 with center at (1,0).</shortdescription>
  </image>
</figure>
<p>
  This region can be described in polar coordinates as <m>0\leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{4}</m> since the point <m>(x,y)=(1,1)</m> is <m>(r,\theta) = (1, \pi/4)</m> in 
  polar coordinates. And <m>\sec(\theta) \leq r \leq 2\cos(\theta)</m> since the circle has polar equation <m>r=2\cos(\theta)</m> and the line <m>x=1</m> has polar equation
  <m>r=\sec(\theta)</m>. Note that <m>x^2+y^2 = r^2</m> so our double integral is 
  <md>
    \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \int_{\sec(\theta)}^{2\cos(\theta)} \frac{ r}{(r^2)^2} dr d\theta = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \int_{\sec(\theta)}^{2\cos(\theta)} r^{-3} dr d\theta =
  </md>
  <md>
    \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \left(\left. -\frac{1}{2}r^{-2} \right\vert_{\sec(\theta)}^{2\cos(\theta)}\right) d\theta = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \left(\frac{1}{2}cos^2(\theta) - \frac{1}{8}\sec^2(\theta)\right) d\theta =
  </md>
  <md>
    =\left.\left( \frac{1}{4}(\theta + \frac{1}{2}\sin(2\theta))-\frac{1}{8}\tan(\theta) \right)\right\vert_0^{\pi/4} = \frac{\pi}{16}
  </md>
  
</p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here are two more video examples:
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>Double integral in polar coordinates example 1</caption>
    <video youtube="YQfta_poP1k"/>
  </figure>
