  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-center-of-mass">
    <title>Center of Mass</title>
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integral-applications">
    <title>Applications of Double Integrals</title>
    <p>
      Recall from Math 152, that the center of mass of a region with uniform density is <m>(\bar{x},\bar{y}) = (M_y/M, M_x/M)</m> where <m>M</m> is the mass or area and
      <m>M_y</m> and <m>M_x</m> are the moments of mass. Defining center of mass with double integrals is more straight-forward:
    </p>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-COM">
      <title>Center of Mass</title>
      <p>
        For a region with area <m>A</m> and uniform density:
        <md>
          \bar{x} = \frac{1}{A}\int\int_R x dA, \quad \bar{y} = \frac{1}{A}\int\int_R y dA
        </md>
        Note that with this more general definition, it is easy to convert to polar coordinates and also to generalize to higher dimensions.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the center of mass of the region bounded by <m>y=1-x^2</m> and the <m>x-</m>axis. 
      </p>
      <p>
        A picture is always helpful.
      </p>
      <figure>
    <caption>Our region</caption>
      <image source="assets/comass1.png">
        <description>
        <p>
        a graph where the region between y = 1 minus x squared and the x axis is shaded.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
      <p>
        Recall that the center of mass must lie along line of symmetry. Here the <m>y-</m>axis is a line of symmetry so <m>\bar{x}=0</m>. The area is 
        <md>
          \int_{-1}^1 (1 - x^2)dx = \left. (x - \frac{1}{3}x^3)\right\vert_{-1}^1 = \frac{4}{3]}
        </md>
        The double integral we need to calculate is 
        <md>
          \int_{-1}^1 \int_{0}^{1-x^2} y dy dx = \int_{-1}^1 \left(\left. \frac{y^2}{2}\right\vert_0^{1-x^2}\right)dx =
        </md>
        <md>
          \frac{1}{2}\int_{-1}^1 (1-2x^2+x^4)dx = \left.\frac{1}{2}\left( x - \frac{2}{3}x^3 + \frac{x^5}{5}\right)\right\vert_{-1}^1 = \frac{16}{15}
        </md>
        So <m>\bar{y} = (16/15)/(4/3) = 0.8</m> and <m>(\bar{x},\bar{y}) = (0,0.8)</m>.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      This method is easily adaptable for a non-uniform density.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the center of mass of the region pictured below with density <m>\rho(x,y) = x/(x^2+y^2)</m>.
      </p>
      <figure>
    <caption>Annulus in the first quadrant</caption>
      <image source="assets/comass2.png">
        <description>
        <p>
        An annulus in the first quadrant with outer radius 8 and inner radius 4.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  Mass is density times area or in terms of double integrals <m>M = \int\int_R \rho(x,y) dA</m>. The moments of mass are similarly defined as 
  <md>
    M_y = \int\int_R \rho(x,y) x dA, \quad M_x = \int\int_R \rho(x,y) y dA
  </md>
  In this example it is easier to calculate with polar coordinates. We make the following conversions:
  <md>
    <mrow>\rho(x,y) =\, \amp \frac{r\cos(\theta)}{r^2} = \frac{\cos(\theta)}{r}</mrow>
    <mrow>x=\, \amp r\cos(\theta)</mrow>
    <mrow>y=\, \amp r\sin(\theta)</mrow>
    <mrow>dA=\, \amp r dr d\theta</mrow>
  </md>
  We have there double integrals to calculate over the region <m>4\leq r \leq 8</m> and <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi/2</m>.
  <md>
    <mrow>M=\, \amp\int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 \frac{\cos(\theta)}{r} r dr d\theta = \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 \cos(\theta)dr d\theta=(\sin(\theta)\vert_{0}^{\pi/2})(r\vert_4^8) = 4</mrow> 
    <mrow>M_x=\, \amp \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 \frac{\cos(\theta)}{r}(r\sin(\theta)) r dr d\theta = \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 (r\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta)) dr d\theta </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \left(\left.\frac{r^2}{2}\right\vert_4^8 \right)\left(\left.-\frac{1}{2}\cos^2(\theta)\right\vert_0^{\pi/2}\right) = 12 </mrow>
       <mrow>M_y=\, \amp \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 \frac{\cos(\theta)}{r}(r\cos(\theta)) r dr d\theta = \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_4^8 (r\cos^2(\theta)) dr d\theta </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \left(\left.\frac{r^2}{2}\right\vert_4^8 \right)\left(\left.\frac{1}{2}(\theta+\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta))\right\vert_0^{\pi/2}\right) = 6\pi </mrow>
  </md>
  And the center of mass is
  <md>
    (\bar{x},\bar{y}) = \left(\frac{M_y}{M},\frac{M_x}{M}\right) = \left(\frac{3\pi}{2},3\right)
  </md>
  
</p>
    </example>
